ted membership lists\" suggests each list should be sorted. Also the object keys are the community IDs (verbatim from refinement values).\n\nWe need to produce the members object with keys as community IDs, each containing sorted list of node names. And aggregate_edges: array of [community_u, community_v, weight] sorted in some deterministic order. Usually, sorting by community pair (lexicographically) and perhaps by weight for ties? The schema doesn't specify, but we need to be deterministic. Possibly sort by first community, then second community, then maybe weight? But weight might be same for duplicates after aggregation? Actually we aggregate by summing weights, so only one edge per (comm_u, comm_v) pair (undirected). The canonical order: \"Canonicalize each aggregate endpoint pair so undirected duplicates coincide.\" Means we should treat (comm_u, comm_v) as undirected, so for each undirected pair, we ensure a canonical order: e.g., sort the two community names lexicographically, then sum weights. Then sort the resulting edges by the first community, then second community. Also self-loops: when comm_u == comm_v, produce an edge with that same community for both.\n\nReturn {\"aggregate_edges\": [...], \"members\": {...}}.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) to extract edges and refinement, then compute.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Get edges = case[\"edges\"]; refinement = case[\"refinement\"].\n2. Build a dict mapping community to list of nodes. Iterate over refinement items: node -> comm. Append node to comm list. After building, sort each list.\n3. Build aggregate edges dict: key = (comm_u, comm_v) but canonicalize: if comm_u > comm_v, swap so that (min, max) for undirected. Then sum weights. Use a dict mapping tuple to weight.\n4. For each edge (u,v,w), map u->comm_u, v->comm_v, then canonicalize pair: comm_a, comm_b = sorted([comm_u, comm_v]) (but note: self-loop if same, sorted returns same). Then add w to dict[pair].\n5. Convert dict to list of [comm_a, comm_b, total_weight]. Sort list: first by comm_a, then by comm_b. Since they are strings, sorted() works.\n6. Return {\"aggregate_edges\": edges_list, \"members\": members_dict}.\n\nCheck if there are any additional constraints: \"parent-community constraints\" - not sure but we just follow refinement map given. Deterministic mapping: yes.\n\nEdge cases: If a node is not in refinement? But all nodes in edges should be in refinement per fixture. We'll assume valid input.\n\nOutput format: Must match schema exactly: aggregate_edges items exactly 3 elements: string, string, number. Members: object with dynamic keys from refinement values, each array of strings.\n\nWe need to produce Python code as a string. Use json.dumps to serialize? No, we need to output the source code as a JSON string. So we write a Python string that defines solve(case).\n\nI'll write the code.\n\nAlso note: The \"case\" argument is exactly the payload object. So case is a dict with keys \"edges\", \"refinement\", \"_case_nonce\". We'll use those.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Use only standard library (json maybe for output but not needed).\n\nWrite solve(case) returning dict.\n\nCode:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    # Build members\n    members = {}\n    for node, comm in refinement.items():\n        members.setdefault(comm, []).append(node)\n    for comm in members:\n        members[comm].sort()\n    # Aggregate edges\n    agg = {}\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        cu = refinement[u]\n        cv = refinement[v]\n        pair = (cu, cv) if cu <= cv else (cv, cu)  # canonical order\n        agg[pair] = agg.get(pair, 0) + w\n    # Convert to sorted list\n    aggregate_edges = sorted([list(p) + [w] for p, w in agg.items()], key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": aggregate_edges, \"members\": members}\n\nBut note: We used list(p) to convert tuple to list. The schema expects array (list). That's fine.\n\nCheck: For self-